  "preamble": "Lowering of the nucleus of a front closing diphthong [ei]/[ɛi] to [ai]. It recurs independently across West Germanic: Middle High German ei became New High German [aɪ], the Dutch ij/ei diphthong is lowered to [ai] in Polder Dutch, and the FACE diphthong of Cockney and similar accents has a nucleus as open as [a].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Diphthong Lowering, Nucleus Lowering",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "German",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Middle High German stein 'stone' (ei)",
          "to": "Stein /ʃtaɪn/",
          "note": "The original MHG diphthongs ei, ou, öu were lowered in Early New High German and fell together with the new diphthongs from î, û, iu."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Dutch (Polder Dutch)",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "ij/ei /ɛi/, e.g. kijken 'to look'",
          "to": "[ai]",
          "note": "Among younger speakers in Utrecht, North Holland and South Holland, /ɛi/ is increasingly lowered to [ai]; the change emerged around the 1970s."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "English (Cockney)",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
